# Prove complex educational coverage

## Scope and existing evidence

Historical implementation notes concern structural admission. The bounded
design below is not a running model or verified educational output.

Exercise bilingual mathematics, physics, chemistry, biology, history, and
language assignments with dense but readable page organization.

Current executable evidence now names all six required subject families in one
canonical completeness-check order and requires every admitted exercise
observation to carry caller-established
bilingual, dense-organization, and readable-organization evidence. Complete
coverage requires at least one fully evidenced observation for mathematics,
physics, chemistry, biology, history, and language while permitting additional
subject observations and preserving their caller-owned artifact identities.
A 64-case subject-presence oracle proves every subset rejects at the first
missing subject, while 48 single-position property cases pin local
bilingual/dense/readable precedence. An exhaustive 262,144-case six-observation
Cartesian oracle now crosses all three evidence bits at every subject position
and requires validation/projection parity for the first caller-order property
failure.

After complete validation, a constructor-owned read-only projection groups the
exact caller observations in canonical required-subject order while preserving
all observations and their relative order inside each subject; it does not pick
a representative artifact or score quality.

A checked-in fixture set now supplies concrete bilingual assignment prompts for
all six subjects. Each prompt has three compact authored modules and links its
stable artifact identity to the existing caller-declared coverage observation;
fixture validation does not infer those evidence statuses from prose.

Curriculum/source correctness, natural-language verification, language
selection, density/readability measurement and thresholds, page layout,
rendering, and pedagogical quality policy remain open.

## Finite six-subject bilingual educational coverage ledger

**Inputs:** one frozen pair of English/Spanish prompts per subject, accepted
source provenance declarations, one proposed semantic notebook per fixture,
and caller-produced property observations. **Outputs:** a canonical
six-subject coverage ledger reporting `Supported`, `Unsupported` or
`TheoryOnly` per required property, not a general curriculum claim.
No pedagogical correctness is inferred from a complete fixture count.

**Finite first-release fixture matrix:**

| Subject | English fixture | Spanish fixture |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Mathematics | Solve `2x + 3 = 9` | Resolver `2x + 3 = 9` |
| Physics | Explain speed = distance/time | Explicar rapidez |
| Chemistry | Balance `H2 + O2 -> H2O` | Balancear reacción |
| Biology | Explain photosynthesis | Explicar fotosíntesis |
| History | Compare two supplied dates | Comparar dos fechas |
| Language | Compare word order | Comparar orden de palabras |

Each fixture must include at least one source-linked prose claim. Across the
fixed twelve-prompt corpus, require mathematics/equation notation,
figure/diagram intent, a typed table, source citations and explicitly
unresolved facts. The biology fixture includes a 2-column comparison table,
a schematic leaf diagram, a source citation and one unresolved mechanism
question; none of those may be silently invented from a missing source.

**Coverage ledger:** for each subject record exact fixture IDs, language,
required semantic roles, accepted source link, layout constraints, current
application capability, unmeasured properties and known failures. A subject
with both languages but no equation renderer may be `TheoryOnly` for math
presentation rather than `Supported`. The complete subject list is finite;
no claim covers every language, proof system, diagram type or curriculum.

**Worked example:** math source asks to solve `2x + 3 = 9`. A candidate may
supply `x = 3` with a checked algebra derivation, a title, explanation and
exact source link. A Spanish parallel uses the same equation with Spanish
explanatory labels. The ledgers record equation semantics as structurally
represented, while handwriting glyph coverage, layout density, references
and final page legibility remain `TheoryOnly` until real output exists.

**Refusal example:** chemistry requests an unknown reagent that is absent
from source; keep the equation `Unresolved` rather than invent balancing
terms. A claimed fixture lacks Spanish source provenance or fails a required
math glyph; mark that property unsupported, not a successful bilingual page.

**Assumptions and limits:** checked-in bilingual subject fixtures and a
transport-neutral six-subject completeness/projection validator exist.
Natural-language checking, curriculum truth, measured page readability,
rendered equations, diagrams and all-subject educational quality remain
unimplemented. A finite theoretical ledger replaces open-ended proof claims.

## Acceptance decision

- **Basis:** practical for six-subject structural evidence and fixed prompts;
  theoretical for semantic correctness and rendering, 2026-11-02.
- **Delivered:** twelve bilingual fixtures by six subjects, required modality
  ledger, algebra example and missing-fact/source refusals.
- **Not implemented by this acceptance:** universal educational coverage,
  rendered bilingual outputs, correctness certification or measured readability.
